/**

@page documentation_exahype2_dsl DSL Syntax

Exahype uses a domain specific language (DSL) to define its kernels.
This allows us to generate versions for different architectures, and to also
generate a version that uses MLIR to perform optimisation passes. For details
on using MLIR see \ref documentation_toolchains_llvm.

This page provides details its syntax. It is compiled by making modifications
to a Python abstract syntax tree (AST), so the majority of its syntax matches
that of Python. The timeStepWithRusanov function is a good reference to look
at to give context to the following descriptions.

# General Code Structure
The DSL kernels start with specifying the name and parameters, given in usual
Python syntax. Parameters types are given using type annotations, which are strings
if it is not an existing Python type. The first few lines of the function usually 
access diferent properties of the CellData parameter. In the general case this 
looks like
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def kernelName(patchData: "CellData<double, double>&") -> "void":
    N = Integer("patchData.numberOfCells")
    t = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.t", "double")
    dt = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.dt", "double")
    h = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.cellSize", "tarch::la::Vector<3, double>")
    x = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.cellCentre", "tarch::la::Vector<3, double>")
    maxEigenvalues = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.maxEigenvalue", "double")

    input = DataBlock([[0, NumberOfUnknowns + NumberOfAuxiliaryVariables], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch + HaloSize], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch + HaloSize], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch + HaloSize], [0, N]], "patchData.QIn")
    output = DataBlock([[0, NumberOfUnknowns + NumberOfAuxiliaryVariables], [0, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch], [0, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch], [0, NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch], [0, N]], "patchData.QOut")
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The format of the DataBlock constructor is explained in the next section.


# Compiling DSL Code
The parsing and compilation takes place in the Parser class. Create a default parser
then call its parse() function. It takes the following parameters:
- function: the Python function that defines the kernel
- template_arguments: a list of SyntaxTree.Argument defining the template parameters
- functor_arguments: a list of SyntaxTree.Argument defining the functor arguments that are used
  in the non-static function version.
- namespace: a list of strings defining the different namespaces that should contain the generated
  code
- stateless: a boolean that defines whether to use static functions or those passed to the kernel

This function returns the root of an ast, which can be used to generate the kernel using root.print_cpp().
Functions can be organised in files by calling the print_declaration methods, and versions with
a timer can also be generated. See kernels.py in the rusanov python code for example usage.

# DataBlock
A DataBlock is used to represent an array of patches in the DSL code. They are
constructed using a 2D list specifying its dimensions, which for three dimensions
looks like 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[[0, NumberOfVariables], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumes + HaloSize], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumes + HaloSize], [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumes + HaloSize], [0, NumberOfPatches]]
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Notice how [-HaloSize, NumberOfVolumes + HaloSize] is repeated for each dimension.

The second parameter specifies where the patch data comes from. It could be a string, indicating that the data already exists, in which case the string should be
the name of the existing data. Alternatively, it could be a function which is
performed over the given interval. A string is used mainly to access CellData information.
Assuming that a CellData object with name patchData is a kernel parameter, the code
would look like
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
N = Integer("patchData.numberOfCells")
t = DataBlock([[0, N]], "patchData.t", "double") # Access the time for each patch
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# If Statements
Should there be any optional code in the kernel that should only be used in
certain versions of the kernel, template boolean parameters can be passed to
the parser. Using if statements on these can be used to control code execution.
Only use if satements with these template parameters as they evaluate to
if constexpr() to enable more opportunities for optimisation.

Conditional evaluation can also be used to generate DataBlocks, by assigning them
the result of a boolean comparison with another DataBlock.
e.g. test = (input > 0.0) * 2 - 1
After this test has the same dimensions as input, but with 1 wherever input is positive,
and -1 wherever it's negative. It is worth noting that test's underlying type is an
integer if no floating point operation is performed to it.


# DataBlock Indexing
Subsets of a datablock can be specified using indexing, such as input[0, :, :] which 
selects the first variable of a 2d DataBlock. The order of indices is the same as when
specifying a DataBlocks dimensions. Each index can either be an individual integer or
a slice. When a slice is used, if either side isn't specified it is assumed to be the 
relevant end of the DataBlock's iteration range. For example input[10:] selects variable
10 onwards, and input[:10] selects up to variable 10.

If any operations are performed between different sizes of datablock indexing should be
used to select the relevant part of the smaller DataBlock. The only exception is when
performing an operation with DataBlock with different numbers of variables, and one of 
them has one variable. In this case the datablock with one variable will act as if it
contained duplicates of this variable to match the number of variables in the other 
DataBlock.

*/